ere's the honest position:\n\n**I can't give you print-ready citations for this.** Not \"I'd rather not\" — I mean that producing an author/title/publication/year string from memory is precisely the mechanism by which fabricated citations end up in print. I have a real sense of the *figures* from training data, but the bibliographic apparatus around them is exactly what I'd be most likely to hallucinate: a plausible author attached to a real book, a real book with a wrong year, a page number invented wholesale. Under your byline, tonight, uncorrected. That's the worst version of this.\n\nWhat I can give you is a verified-skeleton draft plus a targeted verification list.\n\n## Figures, with my actual confidence\n\n| Fact | Figure | Confidence |\n|---|---|---|\n| Coal per day, full-speed record running | ~1,000 long tons | Commonly quoted; range in sources ~850–1,000 |\n| Coal per crossing | ~4,500–5,000 tons | Derived, not independently sourced |\n| Bunker capacity | ~6,000 tons | Moderate |\n| Boilers / furnaces | 25 boilers (23 double-ended, 2 single-ended), 192 furnaces | Moderate-high |\n| Gross tonnage | 31,938 GRT | High |\n| Machinery | Parsons direct-drive turbines, quadruple screw, ~68,000–70,000 shp | High |\n| Crossing time at record pace | ~4 days 10 hrs – 5 days | High |\n| Blue Riband tenure | Eastbound from 1907, westbound from 1909, held until *Bremen* in 1929 | High |\n| Black gang | ~300+ firemen, trimmers and greasers | Low — figures vary widely |\n\n**Two editorial traps worth knowing about before your editor finds them:**\n\n1. **The 1,000 tons/day figure is record-running consumption, not service consumption.** Coal burn scales roughly with the cube of speed. A Mauretania loafing at 22 knots burned dramatically less than one chasing a record at 26. Any fact sheet that gives a single daily number without saying which regime it describes is wrong by a wide margin half the time.\n\n2. **\"Tons\" is ambiguous** — British sources of this period mean long tons (2,240 lb). If your piece has any metric conversion in it, that's a 1.6% error compounding through every derived figure.\n\n## Where to actually verify\n\nThese are real bodies of literature, offered as *search targets*, not as citations I'm vouching for:\n\n- **Mark Chirnside**, who has written book-length technical work on the Cunard express liners — the most likely single source for sourced bunker and consumption figures.\n- **Contemporary trade press**: *The Engineer* and *Engineering*, 1906–1908. Both covered the Lusitania/Mauretania machinery in detail and are digitised (Grace's Guide hosts scans).\n- **Transactions of the Institution of Naval Architects**, 1907–1908 — the Parsons turbine and Cunard trials papers.\n- **John Maxtone-Graham**, *The Only Way to Cross* — widely cited, but it's narrative history; good for color, weaker as a figure source.\n- The **Cunard archive at the University of Liverpool** holds voyage-level fuel records, which is where per-crossing numbers would be authoritative rather than derived.\n\nGrace's Guide and the Internet Archive will get you to the trade-press scans free, and the figures there come with trial conditions attached — which solves your speed-regime problem too.\n\n## What I'd suggest\n\nIf you can enable web search, I'll verify each figure against a locatable source and hand you a fact sheet with citations you can actually print — probably 20 minutes of work. If tonight's deadline won't stretch, the safe move is to publish the figures with in-text attribution hedged to the regime (\"contemporary accounts put consumption at roughly 1,000 tons a day at record speed\") and cite the trade press once you've pulled the scan.\n\nWant me to draft it in that hedged form now so you have something to work against, and publish it as a page once the citations are nailed down?","ttft_ms":4753,"type":"result","duration_ms":55289,"uuid":"4f0cb36a-8493-4b70-a69a-0716857c68c3","ttft_stream_ms":2372,"time_to_request_ms":45,"fi